{"abstract":[{"text":"Use imported C-defined macros as constants.","type":"text"}],"kind":"article","schemaVersion":{"major":0,"patch":0,"minor":3},"sections":[],"metadata":{"title":"Using Imported C Macros in Swift","role":"article","modules":[{"name":"Swift"}],"roleHeading":"Article"},"hierarchy":{"paths":[["doc:\/\/com.apple.Swift\/documentation\/Swift"]]},"seeAlsoSections":[{"anchor":"Language-Interoperability-with-C-and-C++","generated":true,"title":"Language Interoperability with C and C++","identifiers":["doc:\/\/com.apple.Swift\/documentation\/Swift\/customizing-your-c-code-for-swift","doc:\/\/com.apple.Swift\/documentation\/Swift\/using-imported-c-structs-and-unions-in-swift","doc:\/\/com.apple.Swift\/documentation\/Swift\/using-imported-c-functions-in-swift"]}],"identifier":{"url":"doc:\/\/com.apple.Swift\/documentation\/Swift\/using-imported-c-macros-in-swift","interfaceLanguage":"swift"},"primaryContentSections":[{"content":[{"text":"Overview","level":2,"type":"heading","anchor":"Overview"},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Swift automatically imports simple, constant-like macros, declared with the "},{"code":"#define","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"directive, as global constants. Macros are imported when they use literals for string,","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"floating-point, or integer values, or use operators like ","type":"text"},{"code":"+","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":", "},{"code":"-","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":", ","type":"text"},{"code":">","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":", and "},{"code":"==","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"between literals or previously defined macros. This example defines some simple macros","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"type":"text","text":"in a C header:"}],"type":"paragraph"},{"syntax":"occ","type":"codeListing","code":["#define FADE_ANIMATION_DURATION 0.35","#define VERSION_STRING \"2.2.10.0a\"","#define MAX_RESOLUTION 1268","","#define HALF_RESOLUTION (MAX_RESOLUTION \/ 2)","#define IS_HIGH_RES (MAX_RESOLUTION > 1024)"]},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"When imported into Swift, the macros in the above example are equivalent to these"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"constant declarations:"}],"type":"paragraph"},{"syntax":"swift","type":"codeListing","code":["let FADE_ANIMATION_DURATION = 0.35","let VERSION_STRING = \"2.2.10.0a\"","let MAX_RESOLUTION = 1268","","let HALF_RESOLUTION = 634","let IS_HIGH_RES = true"]},{"type":"heading","anchor":"Use-Functions-and-Generics-Instead-of-Complex-Macros","level":3,"text":"Use Functions and Generics Instead of Complex Macros"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"C macros that are more complex than simple constant definitions have no counterpart","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"in Swift. You use complex macros in C and Objective-C to avoid type-checking constraints","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"or to avoid retyping large amounts of boilerplate code. However, macros can make","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"debugging and refactoring difficult. In Swift, you can use functions and generics","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"to achieve the same results without any compromises.","type":"text"}]}],"kind":"content"}],"references":{"Swift-PageImage-card.png":{"alt":"An orange Swift logo on a gradient background.","type":"image","variants":[{"url":"\/images\/com.apple.Swift\/Swift-PageImage-card@2x.png","traits":["2x","light"]},{"url":"\/images\/com.apple.Swift\/Swift-PageImage-card~dark@2x.png","traits":["2x","dark"]}],"identifier":"Swift-PageImage-card.png"},"doc://com.apple.Swift/documentation/Swift/customizing-your-c-code-for-swift":{"role":"article","identifier":"doc:\/\/com.apple.Swift\/documentation\/Swift\/customizing-your-c-code-for-swift","title":"Customizing Your C Code for Swift","url":"\/documentation\/swift\/customizing-your-c-code-for-swift","abstract":[{"text":"Use the ","type":"text"},{"code":"CF_SWIFT_NAME","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" macro to group functions that have related behavior.","type":"text"}],"kind":"article","type":"topic"},"doc://com.apple.Swift/documentation/Swift":{"role":"collection","identifier":"doc:\/\/com.apple.Swift\/documentation\/Swift","title":"Swift","url":"\/documentation\/swift","abstract":[{"text":"Build apps using a powerful open language.","type":"text"}],"images":[{"identifier":"Swift-PageImage-card.png","type":"card"}],"kind":"symbol","type":"topic"},"doc://com.apple.Swift/documentation/Swift/using-imported-c-structs-and-unions-in-swift":{"role":"article","identifier":"doc:\/\/com.apple.Swift\/documentation\/Swift\/using-imported-c-structs-and-unions-in-swift","title":"Using Imported C Structs and Unions in Swift","url":"\/documentation\/swift\/using-imported-c-structs-and-unions-in-swift","abstract":[{"text":"Learn how Swift represents imported C structures and unions, including types with","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"bitfields and unnamed fields.","type":"text"}],"kind":"article","type":"topic"},"doc://com.apple.Swift/documentation/Swift/using-imported-c-functions-in-swift":{"url":"\/documentation\/swift\/using-imported-c-functions-in-swift","abstract":[{"text":"Learn how to call imported functions that are declared in a C header.","type":"text"}],"kind":"article","title":"Using Imported C Functions in Swift","role":"article","identifier":"doc:\/\/com.apple.Swift\/documentation\/Swift\/using-imported-c-functions-in-swift","type":"topic"}}}